---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import MazeTool from "../tools/MazeTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="maze">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        A maze at any size, with exactly <strong class="text-slate-300">one route</strong> between any two points.
        Walls engraved, the piece cut out around them, and a seed so the maze you liked is the maze you get back when
        you come to change the corridor width.
    </Fragment>

    <MazeTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </MazeTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">A maze is engraved, never cut</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    This is the one thing the tool will not let you get wrong, so it is worth saying why. A wall here
                    is a <em>line</em>. Cut a line and you get a slot with nothing holding either side of it — cut a
                    maze's walls and what comes off the bed is not a maze, it is a pile of loose rectangles. So the
                    walls go out as line engraving, only the outline is cut, and there is no switch to change that.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-5 grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Perfect, by construction</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The generator stands in a cell, walks to a random neighbour it has not been to, knocks the wall
                        down behind it, and when it is boxed in backs up to the last cell that still had a way out.
                        What that produces is a <strong class="text-slate-300">perfect maze</strong>: exactly one route
                        between any two cells, no loops, no unreachable corners. It is the kind everybody means by
                        "maze", and the only kind whose difficulty is honestly described by its size.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Loops look harder and solve easier</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The <strong class="text-slate-300">loops</strong> control opens some dead ends up. People like
                        the look, so it is offered — but it is labelled for what it does rather than sold as
                        difficulty. In a perfect maze you can rule a corridor out by having been down it; once there
                        are loops you cannot, which sounds harder and in practice means blundering through works.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The seed is a real control</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Without one, dragging the corridor width would reshuffle every wall and the maze on screen
                        would never be the maze you exported. The same seed always gives the same walls. Press
                        <em>another maze</em> until you like one, then change everything else freely.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The answer is not in the file</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">The way through</strong> beside the grid draws the shortest
                        route on the canvas. It is a reading aid and it is in no export, for the obvious reason:
                        engraving the answer onto the puzzle would be a strange thing to hand somebody. If you want a
                        solution card, take a screenshot.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Merged runs, for the head's sake</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A 40 × 40 maze has some three thousand wall segments. Left as separate lines that is three
                        thousand pen-down/pen-up pairs, and the head spends longer travelling than burning. Collinear
                        neighbours are merged into one line each — the status bar shows both counts — which on a big
                        maze more than halves the job and is invisible in the result.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Corridor width is the number that decides whether it is engraveable at all: below about 3 mm the walls
                run into each other and the whole thing comes out as a grey block. If you want a small maze, use fewer
                cells rather than narrower ones.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
